# A job is finished, not started

The owner's report, recorded 2026-10-02, after nearly two slots: **"we get a raw app that can do only
one thing."** His examples are exact. A teacher's gradebook creates, edits and deletes students and
nothing else. A calculator calculates and nothing else. **"Where is the saving, where is the search,
where are the engineering functions?"** He is not asking for more features for their own sake: he is
saying that every app the factory has handed him is technically functional and *still not something a
professional would keep*. His words: *"I want quality apps, and quality matters to me more than the
feature count."*

This is a defect with a name and measured evidence, in `docs/research/2026-10-02-app-depth-anatomy.md`
and `docs/research/2026-10-02-user-review-depth-signals.md`:

- **The name is partial flow.** The middle of the user's job is implemented; the handoffs are not. The
  app is usable and cannot be *finished* with.
- **Measured**: **15.13 %** of low-star reviews are *missing feature* (Khalid 2014), and roughly
  **17–23 %** of all reviews in the mined corpora are feature requests. Users are not complaining that
  the core act is wrong; they are complaining that the job stops.
- **The ranked extras** users name, in evidence order: **export / backup / data portability** — the
  only one that is *itself a paid product* in four commercial apps and a named upgrade reason in a
  paying app's 5★ reviews — then sync, search/filter, reminders, widgets, competitor import, bulk
  operations, statistics, templates.
- **Counter-evidence is real and this rule obeys it**: a shipped extra can destroy an app (a cloud-sync
  race wiped saves and dropped a top-3 game to #13), and an unused feature charges maintenance rent.
  The decision rule the evidence gives is *"what does this replace for users who never asked?"* and
  *"under ~5 % engagement for a month is a removal candidate"* — so depth means the handoffs the job
  actually has, never a settings screen full of switches.

## Why the factory ships partial flow — the mechanism, measured

The scope of a slot **is its promise list**. `differentiators.json` is what the builder is obliged to
deliver, and every promise must reach `shipped` with a proof. A contract that names only the middle of
the job therefore produces exactly an app that does only the middle, and it does so *while every gate
reports PASS*.

Two measured instances, in this repository:

- `apps/2026-09-26-teacher-gradebook` carries a **`core` promise `edit-every-record`** whose proof is
  `deleteAssignment`. A `core` promise was spent on the record-keeping middle, so the middle is what
  was built.
- `apps/2026-10-01-lawn-round-log` builds `domain/Csv.kt` — and there is **no `ACTION_SEND`, no
  `ACTION_CREATE_DOCUMENT` and no `FileProvider` anywhere in its `app/src/main`**. The CSV is
  constructed and the user has no way to get the file off the phone. The slot's own idea text promised
  "CSV export"; no promise carries it, so no proof was required to exist.

That is the whole defect. It is not that the builder was lazy — it is that **nothing asked for the
handoff**, and the budget rule (`ideation-gate.mdc`: *prefer the cheapest shape*, *cut scope*) then
removed any reason to volunteer one.

## The rule

Every slot decomposes the user's job into **before**, **during** and **after** the core act, and the
contract must cover all three. The job is not the act; the job is everything the act sits inside.

| Stage | What it is | For the gradebook, for the calculator |
|---|---|---|
| **before** | what the user brings or prepares — last term's data, a list they already have, a template, defaults | import a class list; remember the last expression type |
| **during** | the central act the app is named for | record a grade; evaluate an expression |
| **after** | what the user does with the result — keeps it, sends it, files it, is reminded of it, reviews it | export the term a parent can read; save/share the result |

**At least one `shipped` claim must be a handoff** — a claim whose `depthFamily` is a *connective*
family and whose proof artifact actually contains that family's API. A contract of three core acts and
no connective is the defect this rule exists to stop.

### The depth families

Each claim declares its `depthFamily` from this closed set. The **connective** ones are the handoffs;
they are the ones that make a job finishable, and they are the ones a proof can be checked against.

| `depthFamily` | What it is | Proof token the artifact must contain | Counts toward the handoff floor? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `core_operation` | the app's central act | — | **never** — this is the middle, not a differentiator |
| `data_in` | bring the user's own data, media or an incumbent's export in | `ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT`, `ACTION_GET_CONTENT`, `GetContent`, `OpenDocument`, `PickVisualMedia`, `ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE`, `TakePicture`, `ImageCapture`, `ClipData`, `ClipboardManager` | yes |
| `data_out` | get the result off the device usable | `ACTION_SEND`, `ACTION_CREATE_DOCUMENT`, `FileProvider`, `PdfDocument`, `share` | yes |
| `durability` | the user cannot lose the work | `dataExtractionRules`, `fullBackupContent`, `UndoState`, `undo`, `rememberSaveable`, `SavedStateHandle` | yes |
| `reminder` | the app reaches the user | `NotificationChannel`, `WorkManager`, `NotificationManagerCompat` | yes |
| `widget` | capture without opening the app | `GlanceAppWidget`, `AppWidgetProvider` | yes |
| `find` | find it again later | `SearchBar`, `Fts4`, `Fts5`, `MATCH`, `searchQuery` | yes |
| `bulk` | act on many at once | `selectAll`, `toggleSelection`, `multiSelect`, `selection` | yes |
| `insight` | see the whole picture | `Canvas`, `Vico`, `chart`, `statistics`, `aggregate` | **no** — the research rates this presence-only |
| `speed`, `fit`, `access`, `collaboration`, `trust` | templates, personalisation, accessibility, sharing, no lock-in | — | **no** — reportable, never floored |

**A camera capture is `data_in`; a playback is not.** The owner's decision, recorded 2026-10-02, made
the phone's **free, local** capabilities legitimate — the photo picker, the gallery, the user's own
media, the motion sensors and the camera, with no server and no paid API. The picker
(`PickVisualMedia`) was already a token; the camera was not, which left the gate unable to count the
handoff a camera-shaped job actually is. The camera brings the user's own content *into* the app exactly
as the picker does — only the door differs — so it joins `data_in` with its own tokens:
`ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE` (the system camera intent), `TakePicture` (the
`ActivityResultContracts.TakePicture` route) and `ImageCapture` (the CameraX use case). Three boundaries
are stated rather than implied, so nothing is forced into a family it does not fit. **Playing the user's
own media is not a `data_in` handoff**: nothing crosses the app's boundary, so it is either the act the
app is named for (`core_operation`, never floored) or an on-device rendering, and neither finishes the
job. **Recording the user's own media is likewise the act itself**; the recording becomes a handoff only
when the result leaves the device, which is `data_out`. **Motion sensors are in the same position as
playback** — they feed the act rather than cross the app's boundary — so they take no family of their
own. The media picker needs no new token: `PickVisualMedia` already carries it.

### The schema, added to `differentiators.json`

```jsonc
{
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "term-export",
      "weight": "core",
      "jobStage": "after",            // before | during | after
      "depthFamily": "data_out",      // from the closed set above
      "promise": "...",
      "evidence": { /* unchanged */ },
      "status": "shipped",
      "proof": { /* unchanged; its artifact must contain the family's token */ },
      "comparison": { "incumbent": "<package or title>", "limitation": "<what it does not do>", "source": "<path or URL>" }
    }
  ],
  "jobCoverage": {
    "before": { "claimId": "…" },      // or a scout refusal, below
    "during": { "claimId": "…" },      // never a refusal
    "after":  { "claimId": "…" }        // or the owner's refusal, below
  }
}
```

`before` and `after` may carry a refusal **instead of** a claim. `before` is the scout's call, with a
reason: what the user brings or prepares is a fact about the job the scout measured, and its own words
are enough.

```jsonc
"before": { "refusal": { "reason": "…", "decidedBy": "scout", "recordedAt": "2026-10-02" } }
```

**`during` is never refusable.** It is the act the app is named for; refusing it means there is no app.

**`after` is the stage this rule exists for, and refusing it is the owner's decision.** The handoff is
the single most valuable capability this contract promises — it is what makes the job finishable, and the
research in this rule's own opening ranks export / backup / data portability first — so refusing `after`
is not a scheduling note a scout writes; it is a decision to ship without the handoff. It therefore needs
**the owner's own words**, exactly as weakening a `core` promise does: `decidedBy` must be `"owner"`, and
the refusal carries the same `ownerDecision { words, recordedAt }` a narrowed or dropped `core` promise
carries, validated by the **same helper** so the two paths cannot drift apart. A refusal recorded by the
scout, or a missing or empty owner record, is a `FAIL` naming what is missing — *"I did not think of it"*
was never a reason, and it is not the scout's reason to give.

```jsonc
"after": {
  "refusal": {
    "reason": "…",
    "decidedBy": "owner",
    "recordedAt": "2026-10-02",
    "ownerDecision": { "words": "<the owner's own words>", "recordedAt": "2026-10-02" }
  }
}
```

## What is banned

- A job contract with **no connective claim** — three ways to edit a record and no way to get it out.
- A `core` promise whose content is a basic record operation. `edit-every-record` is not a reason to
  install; it is the middle of the job wearing a promise's clothes.
- A **proof that does not contain the family's token**. A `data_out` claim proved by a class that
  builds a CSV string and an app with no share intent is the `lawn-round-log` defect: the artifact
  exists, the handoff does not.
- Declaring `insight` (`chart`/`statistics`) to satisfy the floor. The research rates it presence-only.
- A refusal written to make a gate green. A `before` refusal is the scout's words for why the job has no
  such stage; an `after` refusal is the **owner's** words for giving up the handoff, and a scout's refusal
  there is refused by the gate. Neither is a formality.
- Padding the list with features the job does not have. The counter-evidence is in this rule's own
  opening: an unused feature costs more than it earns.

## What it can prove, and what it cannot

**Machine-checked:** the schema; that every stage resolves to a claim that exists; that `during` is
never a refusal; that a `before` refusal carries a reason, a decider and a date while an `after` refusal
is the owner's, in his own words; that at least one `shipped` connective claim exists; and that its proof
artifact contains the named token for its family. A refusal's `reason` is free text under the same
"not a placeholder" rule as every other field, which rejects a token but cannot read sincerity — that
limit is why a human audits every refusal.
Check ids: **`slot.differentiators`** and the fixture suite in `scripts/core/policy.mjs`.

**Human-audited, reported and never auto-passed:** the CRUD report — claims whose text reads as a bare
record operation — every refusal, and every `comparison`. The CRUD predicate is a **report, not a
gate**: `docs/research/2026-10-02-depth-encoding-and-detectability.md` measured that the repo's own
worst case (`edit-every-record`) escapes a lexical predicate because it carries a contrast clause, so a
fail here would be a false positive on correct prose. It prints, and the owner reads it at checkpoint 1.

**This cannot prove the app is good, wanted or worth paying for** — and it is gameable: three invented
claims plus one `ACTION_SEND` satisfies it. What it removes is the *unnoticed* partial flow: the slot
whose contract never mentioned the handoff, so nobody ever asked whether the job could be finished.

**The honest limit**, in this factory's own terms: a rule that only a document asserts is a rule nobody
follows. The normative text is here; the enforcement is `slot.differentiators` under
`scripts/core/policy.mjs`; and applicability is **derived, not hand-listed**
(`scripts/core/baseline.mjs`), so the slots that predate this rule carry their findings as a printed
note while every slot from today is gated. The judgement that separates a rich app from a padded one
stays with the owner, at checkpoint 1.

